# Feature workflow

Normative product requirements are human/project-owner controlled. Never modify them solely to make
a failing or incomplete implementation appear compliant.

1. Select exactly one requested `READY` feature, or run `python scripts/feature_registry.py
   next-ready` and stop on `NO_READY_FEATURE`. Do not implement a `BACKLOG` item.
2. Read the selected specification's normative requirements and acceptance criteria. Do not read
   every feature specification.
3. Understand the requested behavior without changing product intent. If ambiguity materially
   affects behavior, mark the feature `BLOCKED`, record the exact question in the agent-maintained
   implementation record, run `sync`, and stop.
4. Localize the current implementation and relevant tests by searching before broad reading.
5. Assess callers, data flow, dependencies, and documentation impact.
{% if profile == "minimal" -%}
6. Identify the smallest owning module; introduce a new boundary only when the feature needs it.
{% else -%}
6. Identify the owning layer or domain before editing; preserve the **{{ profile }}** dependency
   direction.
{% endif -%}
7. Define verification before implementation.
8. Change only `Status: READY` to `Status: IN_PROGRESS`, then run `sync`.
9. Implement the minimum coherent change without speculative layers or dependencies.
10. Run the targeted behavior test, then the required repository gates.
11. Review the final diff against the unchanged normative requirements.
12. Update only the agent-maintained implementation record with result, files, dependencies,
    verification, design decisions, deviations/blockers, and follow-up candidates. Do not implement
    follow-ups opportunistically.
13. Change `IN_PROGRESS` to `DONE` only after all acceptance criteria and gates pass, run `sync`, run
    `check`, and stop. Do not automatically begin another feature.

The implementing agent may transition only `READY → IN_PROGRESS`, `IN_PROGRESS → DONE`, or
`IN_PROGRESS → BLOCKED`. If a current requirement cannot be met, record why, mark `BLOCKED`, sync,
and stop; do not rewrite the requirement.

Specification authoring is a separate task. Only when explicitly asked may an agent run `new` or
help structure product-owner text. A scaffold remains `BACKLOG` unless the owner explicitly approves
it as `READY`; the authoring task does not proceed into implementation automatically.
